this barren wasteland is one of the mine  dumps found in and around South Africa's  biggest city Johannesburg the soil here  is full of toxins like arsenic lead and  radioactive uranium the heavy rains and  dust storms that have become more  frequent in recent years have spread the  contamination  especially around Soweto and its  neighboring townships that's led Davila  Mokoena to become an environmental  activist educating people about the  health hazards posed by the mine dumps  she's invited us to a family home in  Soweto where her older sister and two  nephews live Davila knows that here  environmentalists use like climate  change are also social issues climate  change largely is viewed as an  environmental issue however it  encompasses everything it's a  developmental issue it's a it's a human  rights issue it's a social issues and  you find that when we look at the  impacts of climate change and how one  can adapt the most vulnerable are the  women and children  Divi Lee grew up under the racist  apartheid regime back then mining debris  was dumped directly next to the  townships where black people lived at a  safe distance from affluent white  suburbs as a young girl she saw how much  more vulnerable the poor and  disadvantaged were to environmental  problems women suffered doubly not only  were they exposed to the hazards in a  deeply entrenched patriarchal society  women have fewer choices and fewer means  to protect themselves that men could  dictate a woman's fate was a notion to  really grow up with traditionally you  know when a man gets married they're  looking to get a boy as a firstborn but  when I came out and they found that it's  a girl then my father said I've had  enough of kids that's the minimum of my  name Davila it means I've had enough  Davila was the third of four daughters  her father was a proud man who took good  care of his family yet he was also a  deeply traditional man he saw no value  in educating girls I thought I told my  mom did him he's not going to take again  to university because he feels he's  wasting his money because we will go to  school then start working but we'll be  working for another family because we'll  get married and work for someone else or  another feeling so he'll rather involved  invests in boys rather than in girls so  that things you know I think it sort of  inspired me to look into these gender  issues  Davi Lee did study anyway earning the  money she needed as a bank teller and  later as a marketer at a big national  newspaper she got more and more involved  in community work eventually she quit  her job and organized environmental  cleanup campaigns in Soweto  today Davila is the South Africa  coordinator for the gender into urban  climate change initiative the global  network evaluates and monitors the  gender components of climate protection  strategies in cities around the world  that a project workshop in Johannesburg  the coordinators share the experiences  and challenges faced by women in their  countries in India for instance many  poor families can't afford the security  of a basic life insurance Mumbai is of  course to Lydia  okay so fishing is a big industry there  so whenever the men go out to sea and  whenever there are storms and they don't  return back the women have either to  relocate or to look out for a different  business and to care for the family  themselves so the burden on them is  increased ten times more in South Africa  the initiative works together with two  cities swanny and Johannesburg Divi Lee  is especially interested in introducing  climate smart urban agriculture in  johannesburg Shubert park an abandoned  greenhouse has now become a community  garden the cooperative grows vegetables  and medicinal herbs currently the  project provides work for two formerly  jobless women two ladies the owner firm  in their area where they stay and then  they come here as volunteers to the  greenhouse to take care of their space  with the changing climate agricultural  practices have to change too the project  teaches women farmers how to adapt  sustainably in order to ensure income  and food security in the future so yeah  as we doing organic vegetables it helps  us to sustain ourselves because we sell  ours organic vegetables today local  market so we are able to have something  every week making themselves heard by  policymakers remains a big challenge the  city government had agreed to renovate  the greenhouse but cut the funding at  the last minute it's still a long way to  go but we will keep on raising our voice  and keep on commenting and critiquing  their their policies and make  recommendations identify gaps where  there are gifts it's work that takes  strength and perseverance and dávila  Mokoena  has plenty of both
 

